Date: Wed, 24 Feb 1993 01:18:05 -0800 (PST)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: EXT: Kids vs. Uploading

Why cut off possibilities?  I hope to immortalize myself both
the current way (via passing on my memes and genes to
descendants) and the future ways (via life extension,
cryonics, uploading, copying, etc.)  In fact, the two goals overlap.
My two major short-term life extension goals are to be fertile
and of sharp mind at age 80, so that I can take advantage
of the best technology of that era (gene therapy?  neural
transplant? cell repair? demonstrated reversible cryonics?  etc.) 
for life extension and have plenty of indirect immortality living 
in my descedants.

Having children and life extension costs must be
compared and traded off.  Some of the tradeoffs are
direct, eg should I name the children or Alcor on my life
insurance policy?  However there may also be synergy, eg 
relatives there to put me into and get me out of cryonic
suspension, and look after me in the meantime.  It may
be worthwhile to get two fat policies, if I can afford it!

Stephen Withrow:
> I want to live on
> personally, physically, in whatever mind and body is available to me.

But are you living on "personally" and "physically" if you upload?
Uploading involves destroying the entire body and just making a copy 
of the memes; raising children involves merging genes and copying
the memes in a rather error-prone but flexible manner.  

Hopefully uploading will be far more accurate than raising children.  
Having children involves major memetic and genetic mixup; successful 
uploading will involve tossing over many if not most of our biological 
processes (eating, sex, sleeping, etc.) which play a dominant role in 
our current "identities".  Even if we can have "virtual sex", "virtual 
eating", etc. these will be inefficient and losing propositions in a 
competitive culture of uploads and other forms of software life.  

Even in the era of uploads it might be advantageous to make copies 
of merged identities, a process similar to raising children.  Today and 
for the next few to several hundred decades, uploading is not an option 
and it makes sense to proceed with boundless expansion in the environment 
we now find ourselves in.

> It's a fact of life and death that all sentient, primitive, 
> genetically-evolved carbon-based lifeforms are programmed by their 
> DNA to 'want' to make themselves expendable by creating their 
> replacements

Even with "want" in quotes that's stretching it.  We're programmed
to want sex, which frequently led to having children until we
learned how to short-circuit the process.  Demographics and examination 
of our culture indicate there is very little desire for children
themselves, compared to the desire for sex.   Also, having children
does not make one "expendable"; people with children live just as
long as those without (give or take some risk factors for having or 
not having children, which are minor and roughly balance out in developed
countries), and I can't imagine they would be any more prone to
want to die.

Instinct having been circumvented, the only remaining motivation for 
having children is an extropian kind of memetic motivation, a desire 
to do the kind of uploading we can do today, a desire for the boundless 
expansion of one's memes and genes.

Nick Szabo				szabo@techbook.com

