From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: EXT: Selective breeding

drw:
> I would hazard that the present human lifespan is optimal
> (in the sociobiological sense), and that attempts to give people the
> option of living longer will prove futile, as our genes counter-attack
> the memes of longevity.

Genetic evolution doesn't stand a chance.  Memes have already 
short-circuited the most important pathway of the genes, sex, with 
birth control.  We are experimenting with simple somatic gene therapy 
(and not experimenting with human gene-line therapy only because 
of the prevailing  anti-extropian medical "ethics").  When
we have deciphered the workings of the brain, we will be
able to change the most basic motivations of humans via
gene-line therapy, if desired.  For example, we could
eliminate or radically alter the two strongest human motivations, 
sex and sleep.  A superior transhuman might get an orgasm
as a reward for deep or rapid intellectual thought, and be able 
to operate at full brain capacity 24 hours a day.  If there
is a genetic "aging clock" we can turn it off while boosting
the production of free-radical scavengers like glutathione
and SOD.  

Also, longevity per se does not lead to fewer children.
In the short term there may be tradeoffs (eg naming Alcor 
vs. naming children as beneficiary of life insurance policy), 
but there is also synergy.  Descendants can assist one in living 
longer. Having a longer fertility span, longer time to accumulate
wealth, and longer time to stay in contact with descendants
allows an increased quantity and quality of child raising.

