Date: Wed, 24 Feb 93 08:24:14 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: LAW: Justification

Tim Starr writes:

>My dispute with Tom [about justification] seems to hinge upon terms, >unfortunately.  

I think our dispute involves something deeper and more interesting, too,
however.  I was disappointed that we got sidestracked into terminology, as
I think Tim's position has more to credit it than merely its use of words. 
Here is how I reconstruct our positions:

Tom:  The justification of an institution or practice applies only relative
to one to whom it would apply.  Statism can thus be justified relative to
those who consent to it.

Tim:  That doesn't make sense!  Statism is defined as institutional
coercion, and you cannot consent to coercion.

Tom:  But we must recognize that the same institutions that are statist to
some may be consented to by others.

Tim' [hypothetically]:  But because you prefer to ask about the
applications of statism, shouldn't you say that a statist institution can
act in a non-statist ways towards those who consent to its jurisdiction?

Tom:  Yes, I guess so.  But this is just to say that a statist institution
can be justified!

Tim':  Ah!  Now you are focusing on institutions to the exclusion of
practices.  Didn't you once say that you prefered to say "statism" and
"statists" rather than "State" so that you could constantly draw attention
to the fact that individuals--not institutions--are responsible for
institutional coercion?  By using "statist institution" you are letting
"State" back in through the back door!

Tom:  Look, I've never said statist institutions don't exist; I merely want
to emphasize that they depend on individual choices.  We cannot talk
reasonably about statism unless we speak about institutions (though I still
don't like the Hegelian-flavored "State").  And we have to recognize that
the same institution can act in a statist fashion toward some and in a
non-statist fashion towards others.  So long as it claims a right to act in
the latter way, the institution deserves to be called "statist".  I thus
conclude:  the practices of a statist institution can be justified relative
to those who consent to them.

Tim':  That's incomplete.  You should conclude:  the non-statist practices
of a statist institution can be justified relative to those who consent to
them, but its statist practices *can never* be justified relative to those
who are subjected to them.

Tom:  Yes, that is more accurate, though unweildy.  A shorter version of
this conclusion might be:  Statist practices can never be justified, though
statist institutions might.


