Date: Wed, 24 Feb 1993 07:22:45 -0800
From: D Anton Sherwood <dasher@well.sf.ca.us>
Subject: VOTE: better ways?

Marc Ringuette plants a seed:
> An interesting line of inquiry:  when you must cooperate, what methods 
> are better than voting?  This sounds like it would make a good thread.

Where there is only one issue, probably nothing.  But that is rarely the
case.

Steinhaus's work on fair division may help.  Here is a problem from
"Mathematical Snapshots".  Four equal heirs are to divide a house, a mill, a
car and some cash.  How to portion the indivisible objects fairly?  Answer:
Bidding will both measure share-sizes and maximize utility.  In the table,
the columns are the heirs and the entries are their bids (or linear
functions thereof).

           A        B        C        D
House   $ 6000   *10000*    7000     9000
Mill      3000     2000    *4000*    2000
Car      *1500*    1200     1000     1000

Sum      10500    13200    12000    12000
Share     2625     3300     3000     3000

Value     1500    10000     4000        0
Balance   1125    -6700    -1000     3000

(This was written in 1950.)  "In the above table each person's share is got
by dividing his estimate of the total by 4.  In every row the greatest item
appears in a frame and the corresponding object is attributed to the person
whose name stands above the column.  Thus A gets the car, B the house, and C
the mill.  The values of the objects subtracted from the shares of the
persons invested with them give the claims.  A appears with a claim of
$1125, D with one of $3000, whereas B has a negative claim of $6700, and C a
negative claim of $1000.  This means that B and C have to pay money to the
umpire and A and D have to get money from him:

A  $1125	B  $6700	|	$ 7700
D   3000	C   1000	|	- 4125
 =  4125	 =  7700	|	= 3575

"This computation shows that the payments will leave the umpire with a
surplus of $3575; divided by 4 this gives $893.75 for each inheritor.  Thus
A will get the car and $1125 + $893.75 = $2018.75 cash; B will get the house
and have to pay $6700 - $893.75 = $5806.25 off; C will get the mill and will
have to pay $1000 - $893.75 = $106.25 off; D will get $3000 + $893.75 =
$3,893.75.  Thus everybody will finally get more than his due share of the
inheritance [as measured by the bids; work it out yourself]."

Note that each participant has a motive to value the items honestly.  Too
high, and you risk buying something for more than you value it; too low, and
you don't get paid.

A bidding market could arise for each of a number of issues.  Transaction
costs are likely to be high anyway, though.

