Date: Wed, 24 Feb 93 09:35:37 PST
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: EXT: Kids vs. Uploading

Nick makes the point that competitive pressures may force our uploaded
selves to cut back on "human-style" luxuries like virtual eating and
sex in order to keep up with our more streamlined competitors.  The
current Extropy has an article by Hans Moravec in which he elaborates
on this argument (which he has also made here, and on which Robin has
written as well).  I don't really know how competitive the future world
will be, but if these pressures do exist they will represent a further
force for change which will tend to make our future selves even less
recognizable to the people we are today.

Perry may be right that these issues are largely matters of taste.
But I think there is an inconsistency between having an Extropian desire
for boundless expansion and self-transformation, which implies a commitment
to drastic change, and having a simplistic notion of identity preservation.

Imagine somehow engineering an ant to become a human: perhaps transplanting
its miniscule bit of neural tissue into the middle of a human brain,
then teaching that human just as you would a baby until it grows into a
productive member of society.  Is it still the same identity as the ant?
I would say not.

Yet it's possible (even likely, I'd say) that our future selves will be
as dissimilar to us as we are to the ant.

Putting it bluntly, you're either going to die fast (the regular way) or
die slow (by eventually changing so much that all you are today is
effectively gone).  Dieing slowly is less frightening to minds which are
genetically conditioned to fear death.  But growth and death are two sides
of the same coin.  By committing to growth we agree to the death of the
person we are today, in the hopes that we will be a better person tomorrow.

Hal Finney

