Date: Wed, 24 Feb 93 12:02:30 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: ECON: Cover's ideas

Mike Price writes:
> I wonder, does this tie in
> with Cover's universal or 'potpurri' portfolio Robin mentioned, which
> asymptotically approaches the optimum investment strategy?  Is this some
> type of Gresham's Law in reverse, whereby the good subsets of the
> portfolio outgrow the bad?  If so sounds like such an effect would take
> a long time to significantly change the composition of a portfolio,
> perhaps longer than lifetime of most stocks.

Hal responds:
>I could not find a reference for the universal portfolio that Robin
>mentioned.  The papers I saw were more specific in their advice.  Perhaps
>when Robin gets a break from changing diapers or whatever he can fill us
>in on a reference for this idea.

See: Thomas Cover, "Universal Portfolios", Mathematical Finance, V1 N1
  Jan 1991, p1-29.  

Yes the basic idea is that the good portfolios outgrow the bad.  And
yes this can take a long time if you've made a bad choice of weights
across the portfolios.  But even if you have a pretty good idea of
which will be best, can't hurt to put a little in the other portfolios
as insurance - if they're best you'll win too, eventually.  

On the log vs linear return thread, I have to say it's intuitively
obvious that log is better - but it would take a bit of thinking to
remember what convinced me of this.  If anyone out there really claims
that there values for money are better captured by wanting to max
linear return, we could contruct an arbitrarily large beta instrument
(say a very high threshold price call option on a market index
expiring at a distant future time) and challenge them to invest *all*
their money in such a thing.

